            <ArticleTitle>Mycobacterial coronary arteritis in a heart transplant recipient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1072-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A case of mycobacterial vasculitis in a chronically rejected transplanted heart is described. The coronary arteries were the only vessels involved by mycobacteria, although the patient had a generalized infection. The process of chronic rejection, with persistent injury to the intimal vascular lining of a transplanted organ, may lead to defects in the endothelial cell barrier and thus facilitate infiltration of a vessel wall by acid-fast bacilli.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
